<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sées</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sées is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Normandy, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It grew from the Roman-era centre of the Gallic Sagii and has been the seat of a bishopric since late antiquity. Its Gothic cathedral of Notre-Dame, built mainly in the 13th and 14th centuries, is one of the finest in Normandy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sees.jpg" alt="Sées" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A9es_cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sées lies on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a>, five kilometres from its source and about twenty kilometres north-north-east of Alençon, within the Normandie-Maine Regional Natural Park and the forest of Écouves. It is classed as a Petite Cité de Caractère. The commune covers just over 40 square kilometres, most of it arable land, with meadows and a little forest. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> civitas Sagiensis, the city of the Sagii, a Gaulish tribe that made it their capital. The traditional spelling, Séez, is kept by the Church, whose diocese is headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Séez.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> goes back to late antiquity, and Saint Lain is placed about the fourth century. In the ninth century it was a fortified town and fell prey to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>. It then had two parts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a>, the bishop's borough to the north and the new count's borough to the south. The counts of Alençon took control in 1356. The town was captured and recaptured in the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and his sons, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> it was one of the first towns of Normandy to fall to the English, in 1418.</p><p>Pillaged by Protestants during the Wars of Religion, Sées attached itself to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> in 1589, but voluntarily surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> in 1590. The civil authorities adopted the spelling Sées after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Italian campaign of 1796-97, which brought another, Savoyan Séez into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sées Cathedral occupies the site of three earlier churches and dates from the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries. Its west front carries two open-work spires about 70 metres high, the nave was finished towards the end of the thirteenth century, and the choir is notable for the lightness of its construction. It was declared a monument historique in 1875. Nine other buildings and areas are listed, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s palace of 1778, the former abbey of Saint-Martin, and an eleventh-century castle mound. A museum of religious art, opened in 1969 in the old chapter house, holds over 2,000 pieces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sées” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their exact location is debated, but most scholars place them in the Orne region around <b>Sées</b> or Exmes.</blockquote><a href="/state/esuvii">Esuvii</a></li></ul></section>
